here we will overview the work process we went through as
we designed the model and trained it.
In order, we will explain our model, focusing on major design choices,
 then the data preprocessing pipeline. 

---------------- |1| The model --------------------

# 2.1 Major Design Choices
Residual Blocks: The network utilizes standard residual blocks with two 3x3 convolutions, Batch Normalization, and ReLU activations. A 1x1 shortcut connection is used whenever spatial dimensions or channel counts change.

Global Average Pooling: The network uses Adaptive Average Pooling to reduce the spatial dimensions to 1x1 per channel before the final classifier. This reduces parameters and allows to handle arbitrary input resolutions.

Custom Initialization: Uses Kaiming Normal initialization for convolutional layers, which is optimal for ReLU activations. helps to prevent vanishing/exploding gradients early in training.

Gradient Clipping: Prevents exploding gradients by clipping the norm of the gradients during backpropagation.

# 2.2 Model Variants
three main variants are defined:

BalancedResNet (Default): 4 stages, doubling channels (32, 64, 128, 256), with 2 blocks per stage.
WideResNet: Increases channel capacity (48, 96, 192, 384) for learning more varied feature representations.
DeepResNet: Increases depth to 3 blocks per stage to learn more complex, hierarchical features

---------------- |2| data preprocessing and augmentations ----------------

# Data Splits
The dataset is split into train (80%), dev1 (10%), dev2 (5%), and test (5%).

# Augmentation Strategies
The pipeline implements multiple augmentation tiers, standard, agressive, and extreme, each more
altering then the previous. The idea was to try and see what the model can
handle gradually.
the following augmentations were implemented:

- Resize, random cropping and horizontal flip.
- Color jitter, mild contrast saturation and brightness changes.
- Randomly removes square regions from the image.
- GaussianNoise: Simulates sensor noise.
- ChannelShuffle: Prevents the model from over-relying on specific color channels.
- PatchShuffle: Disrupts spatial coherence, forcing the model to learn local textures.

when we first ran our baseline model, which was a very simplified CNN, we got roughly
40% accuracy, when training on 14000 sample with 10 ephocs and batchsize 128.
we then proceeded to train on the same parameters but with all our augmentations.
we saw an immediate reduction to 30%.